 ## Overview
-MixRT is a hybrid neural representation designed to enable real-time Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) rendering on edge devices by decomposing the scene into three complementary components: a low-quality triangle mesh for coarse geometry, a view-dependent displacement map for surface calibration, and a compressed NeRF model stored in an Instant-NGP hash table for density and color [S1]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Vosh](15540-vosh-voxel-mesh.md) by explicitly leveraging distinct graphics hardware units—rasterizers, texture mapping units, and SIMD units—via WebGL APIs to bypass the computational bottlenecks of dense ray-marching, achieving real-time frame rates where other hybrid approaches fail to do so [S1].
